From 5e0273b2ab732a9906fbea8ba5c60abe71b5e22c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yekai pan <pyk@seas.upenn.edu>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 00:34:57 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] l2r-local: bug 11b: look up drop and acquire glue in symbol
 tables built once

`createDtorIfNotExists` and `emitOwnershipAcquisitionFuncIfNotExists`
(lib/IR/ReussirOps.cpp) built a new `mlir::SymbolTable` of the whole
module on every call, to look up the glue function of a record type, and
the acquire/drop expansion calls them for every `ref.drop`/`ref.acquire`
of a named record it outlines: time quadratic in the size of the module.
Found by building the Std.Http program of bug 11 once its SCCP was fixed:
the second acquire/drop expansion ran for more than 45 minutes, 90% of the
time in `SymbolTable::SymbolTable` (perf).

Give both helpers an optional `mlir::SymbolTableCollection`, whose table
they look up and add the functions they create to (the drop glue, the
drain declaration, the acquire glue); the acquire/drop expansion pass
builds one collection per run and passes it through its patterns. The
outlined acquire glue of a record creates, while its body is built, the
glue of its named [value] members (bug 19): `emitOwnershipAcquisition`
passes the collection on to that creation too, or the member's glue would
be missing from the table and a later lookup would define it a second
time ("redefinition of symbol"). The other callers keep building a table
per call. The functions found and created are the same.

With this and the SCCP budget, the Std.Http program builds to an object
(rrc, loaded machine): 26 minutes of MLIR passes, the second acquire/drop
expansion 84 s.

Test: tests/integration/frontend/cell_value_record_glue_order (a Cell<Pair>
read before a Cell<Quad>; by the adversarial review, RV8C-01).
